<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guan Hanqing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1241–c. 1320</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guan Hanqing was a Chinese playwright of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>, regarded as the foremost writer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaju">zaju</a> drama. Working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a>, he is credited with some sixty plays, of which about eighteen survive, including The Injustice to Dou E, a tragedy of a wronged young widow. His vivid vernacular dialogue and sympathy for ordinary people made him a central figure of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guan-hanqing.jpg" alt="Guan Hanqing" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of USSR · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamp_of_USSR_2262.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Guan Hanqing, whose art names were Yizhai and Yizhaisou, was a dramatist, playwright and poet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> who lived from about 1241 to 1320. He has been described as among the most prolific and highly regarded dramatists of the period, and as the most productive and creative playwright of northern plays.</p><p>Jia Zhongming called him the leader of the Pear Garden. He was fully immersed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of his time and well known among actors and fellow writers, and he is even said to have painted and powdered his face to act on stage. He spent much of his later life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> and also lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Guan is credited with a large number of plays, of which only 18 survive, three in fragments. They were written mostly in the vernacular of the time, and in some cases the attribution is highly disputed. He is also said to have been a master of songs, or sanqu. Of these 57 short songs and 14 song suites survive. Above all he is known for his sympathetic portrayal of the downtrodden. Surviving titles include Saving the Prostitute, the Conference of a Single Dao, in which Lord Guan goes to a feast alone, The Pavilion of Moon-Worship, The Wife-Snatcher, The Riverside Pavilion and The Jade Mirror-Stand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Injustice to Dou E That Touched Heaven and Earth, also called Snow in Midsummer, is one of his most popular plays. Dou Duanyun is sold as a child bride so that her father can pay for a journey to the capital for an official exam, and renamed Dou E. Her husband dies two years into the marriage.</p><p>Zhang Lü'er, who had rescued her mother-in-law, tries to force Dou E to marry him and plans to poison her mother-in-law, but his own father eats the soup and dies. Dou E is framed, tortured and sentenced to death after confessing to spare her mother-in-law. She swears that her innocence will be proved by three events: her blood will rise onto white ribbons, snow will fall in summer and Chuzhou will have drought for three years. All three happen.</p><p>Guan wrote many courtroom dramas in which morality and legality work together. A Butterfly Dream differs. Three brothers accidentally kill a nobleman whose horse trampled their father, and the family is acquitted after each tries to accept blame for the others. The play shows a more flexible and complex view of justice than traditional Confucian law allowed and explores the grey area between moral culpability and lawful justice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guan Hanqing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They are major contributions to Chinese literary culture, with playwrights such as <b>Guan Hanqing</b> and Wang Shifu, author of The Romance of the Western Chamber.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Khanbaliq, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.87,116.33&amp;year=1292" aria-label="Zhongdu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Guan Hanqing"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4759 4156h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4759 4156h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.87,116.33&amp;year=1292">What was here in 1292? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
